diff --git a/README.rst b/README.rst
index 653c216729dd4f9aac68ee375f52587d69981645..454ec8defef3d931feb623a2b24cfb1ad387ed53 100644
--- a/README.rst
+++ b/README.rst
@@ -53,6 +53,42 @@ Run
 Open http://127.0.0.1:5000 in a browser.
 
 
+Upgrade an existing database
+----------------------------
+
+Before running this version against an existing Flaskr SQLite database,
+stop the app and back up the database. Use the same instance configuration
+as the running app, including its ``DATABASE`` setting, then run::
+
+    $ flask --app flaskr upgrade-db
+
+The command adds ``post.status`` in place. Existing posts become
+``published``; post IDs, authors, titles, bodies, timestamps, users, and
+other database objects are preserved. The database enforces ``draft`` or
+``published`` and defaults new rows to ``published``. Running the command
+again is safe and preserves each post's current status, including drafts.
+Restart the app after the command succeeds.
+
+Do not use ``init-db`` to upgrade an existing database: it deletes existing
+posts and users. Keep ``init-db`` for new databases. ``upgrade-db`` reports
+an error when no post table exists and does not initialize or clear tables.
+
+
+Drafts and publishing
+---------------------
+
+Choose Published or Draft when creating or editing a post. Published posts
+appear to everyone. Drafts appear on the author's home page with a Draft
+label; other users and anonymous visitors cannot see them there. The
+author can edit, publish, return a post to draft, or delete it using the
+existing controls. Posts remain sorted by creation time, newest first.
+
+Clients that omit ``status`` when creating a post retain the old behavior:
+the post is published. Omitting ``status`` when updating preserves its
+current state. Any other submitted status, including an empty value, is
+rejected with a validation message and leaves the stored post unchanged.
+
+
 Test
 ----
 
